> Ah, so there is logic behind it.

There sure is.  It has to do with touch typing.  A
touch typist will have his/her index fingers hover
around the f and j keys.  Many keyboards even have
little marks on those two keys so touch typists can
stay oriented without ever looking at the keyboard.
The other fingers of the left hand hover near the
a-s-d keys.  The other fingers of the right hand
hover near the k-l-; keys.  From that point every
key other than numbers is one movement away with
one finger.

Translation - The movement system in Nethack is for
right handed touch typists.  Even though I'm left
handed I appreciate that elligence.  The number pad
and mouse are afterthoughts added as a nod to people
who aren't touch typists.  It makes sense that
Nethack would assume its players are touch typists.
All of the DevTeam are Unix programmers and any
good Unix programmer must do touch typing.  I have
never used the mouse or number pad because I was
already nearly doing touch typing before I ever
encountered Nethack.  The first thing I do when a
new version of Nethack comes out is figure out how to
set the defaults to use touch typing and turn off the
numberpad functions.

